[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-66061"] :question/problem "Find the range of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})-1.$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\le -1,\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- Multiplying the first interval by $\\frac{1}{3}$ and subtracting $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\le -1 \\\\  & \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\le -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})-1\\le -\\frac{1}{3}-1 \\\\  & f(x)\\le -\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second interval by $\\frac{1}{3}$ and subtracting $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\ge 1 \\\\  & \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\ge \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})-1\\ge \\frac{1}{3}-1 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge -\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)\\le -\\frac{4}{3},\\,f(x)\\ge -\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{4}{3}]\\cup [-\\frac{2}{3},\\infty ).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-66062/selection/ed779a1c5bca9e72b278f8a6087a89c84a4e0352ea476f865c81716944c7a7e4" :answer/id #uuid "ea7fbd0a-e4ca-5b8d-a277-dcb2c637ff82" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-0]\\cup [1,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-66062/selection/59e89a377db7e4897e3f2463873002abc8f0e92984e98585086499f14acdfef7" :answer/id #uuid "b8eaf9c3-5baa-50a8-81be-e05759fa97a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{4}{5}]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-66062/selection/13b0b1d589383e0e61c165ef36ac27ceb13f4749043804e9c76f8487c468ca3e" :answer/id #uuid "94056ffb-a25a-5ff5-bd39-f8615c37e2c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-66062/selection/d5d9b11087f48d01fe3bc2f5a6e7094fb856939d90f747f8473b7a9a23826095" :answer/id #uuid "b5f1aaa8-637f-504f-aece-60323fd2869b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-8]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-66062/selection/cb473598b0e26b1eef573b9f851434c8525f9388c905ba0630933306dfbe0e72" :answer/id #uuid "93784874-7b2f-5cbc-b194-9fadc3e5e3b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-8]\\cup [8,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-66062/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b1674ac0-d8f5-5250-bd18-3da2707e75d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-66062/selection/d5d9b11087f48d01fe3bc2f5a6e7094fb856939d90f747f8473b7a9a23826095" :answer-field/choices ["q-66062/selection/ed779a1c5bca9e72b278f8a6087a89c84a4e0352ea476f865c81716944c7a7e4" "q-66062/selection/59e89a377db7e4897e3f2463873002abc8f0e92984e98585086499f14acdfef7" "q-66062/selection/13b0b1d589383e0e61c165ef36ac27ceb13f4749043804e9c76f8487c468ca3e" "q-66062/selection/d5d9b11087f48d01fe3bc2f5a6e7094fb856939d90f747f8473b7a9a23826095" "q-66062/selection/cb473598b0e26b1eef573b9f851434c8525f9388c905ba0630933306dfbe0e72"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-66062" :question/id #uuid "f78b0f00-abf4-5ba2-a6f5-e47b01f3518d" :question/math-academy-id "q-66062" :question/problem "Find the range of the function $f(x)=5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})-3.$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\le -1,\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- Multiplying the first interval by $5$ and subtracting $3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\le -1 \\\\  & 5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\le -5 \\\\  & 5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})-3\\le -5-3 \\\\  & f(x)\\le -8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second interval by $5$ and subtracting $3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\ge 1 \\\\  & 5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\ge 5 \\\\  & 5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})-3\\ge 5-3 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)\\le -8,\\,f(x)\\ge 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-8]\\cup [2,\\infty ).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-66062/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8dbb426-dd34-5686-bde9-3ef9d246b5e5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-66062"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-66071/selection/84cbf3b91a97d6d45739c457d2dbcfbcc74fcc2704e63a72f008f843b1ba217d" :answer/id #uuid "1b5c154f-9bde-5c41-94d3-fd235425290b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+3n\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-66071/selection/85119b326425ecbbd0eb28f7feef67cb699f2478c3e0696071e24737c3c91c65" :answer/id #uuid "b39457fc-d1b9-5ea2-bb3d-948b759c3a58" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-66071/selection/c514943e7c51c1cd0d1a8e7198380b1bcab3de1731739734d05dd9636f95f12b" :answer/id #uuid "139cacce-77e7-5028-9844-38189882985f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{3n\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-66071/selection/7c78b75c38e8f758fa40fdae2e71e41ed4a40c202935f50ee4f9973784360aaa" :answer/id #uuid "1ca53a8a-1b88-5d73-92f7-c3a7d3a55291" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{5n\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-66071/selection/c6c28556fffca695cc9110e89262473c41e936b805923a677a04965d90325b90" :answer/id #uuid "29066276-cd13-57d1-ae56-f489abde8c63" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-66071/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "50ee9aac-3118-5863-b25a-cee61da864d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-66071/selection/c514943e7c51c1cd0d1a8e7198380b1bcab3de1731739734d05dd9636f95f12b" :answer-field/choices ["q-66071/selection/84cbf3b91a97d6d45739c457d2dbcfbcc74fcc2704e63a72f008f843b1ba217d" "q-66071/selection/85119b326425ecbbd0eb28f7feef67cb699f2478c3e0696071e24737c3c91c65" "q-66071/selection/c514943e7c51c1cd0d1a8e7198380b1bcab3de1731739734d05dd9636f95f12b" "q-66071/selection/7c78b75c38e8f758fa40fdae2e71e41ed4a40c202935f50ee4f9973784360aaa" "q-66071/selection/c6c28556fffca695cc9110e89262473c41e936b805923a677a04965d90325b90"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-66071" :question/id #uuid "24f4f8ab-4bd4-54bd-a8d1-e84a41a0a56a" :question/math-academy-id "q-66071" :question/problem "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ consists of all values where $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\ne 0,$ and is given by the expression\n\n$$\nx\\ne n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\ne n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6} & \\ne n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{2x}{3} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & \\ne \\frac{3\\pi }{6}+3n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+3n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{3n\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+3n\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ where $n$ is an integer." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-66071/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ec59516-4ba6-5e62-b26a-bad36ea4448f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-66071"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-66074/selection/e1b82fe0222a958627017f956be70b1dbd9a8f6b240bab1af8943b424dd545ba" :answer/id #uuid "6cab8561-0644-5440-b8c1-2436e66c42f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {90}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-66074/selection/42d02e415a64bbc5bc4a915779931ed8a38011721d15aa240e70686f4a6ca7e3" :answer/id #uuid "1d4ba651-acd3-5302-b420-7cd5dac6e2c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\ne {50}^{∘}+n\\cdot {30}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-66074/selection/e4ad0d7a405fd7a838e91a7386e39f72d5321c4c283958e79fe348f333ceb54e" :answer/id #uuid "a5cb0a2b-5a99-5bd3-9ba9-28c4b1938fcd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {30}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-66074/selection/5e5878aa45b5497fc165d404b24b978b848700ed039766ecabe7bdb70e3d230b" :answer/id #uuid "a1fc413f-4e85-5cdc-abb5-65381d8df7fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\ne {30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-66074/selection/6de8d2105e8c75c965fdd7dbc643ab0ebf288d8662f3da0f4d070ad3c25358f3" :answer/id #uuid "80fcdfd2-fb65-5a15-bda2-1e049725d4fe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\ne {50}^{∘}+n\\cdot {60}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-66074/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f182d070-f5e6-573f-8fb5-47eca6ca7fa2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-66074/selection/6de8d2105e8c75c965fdd7dbc643ab0ebf288d8662f3da0f4d070ad3c25358f3" :answer-field/choices ["q-66074/selection/e1b82fe0222a958627017f956be70b1dbd9a8f6b240bab1af8943b424dd545ba" "q-66074/selection/42d02e415a64bbc5bc4a915779931ed8a38011721d15aa240e70686f4a6ca7e3" "q-66074/selection/e4ad0d7a405fd7a838e91a7386e39f72d5321c4c283958e79fe348f333ceb54e" "q-66074/selection/5e5878aa45b5497fc165d404b24b978b848700ed039766ecabe7bdb70e3d230b" "q-66074/selection/6de8d2105e8c75c965fdd7dbc643ab0ebf288d8662f3da0f4d070ad3c25358f3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-66074" :question/id #uuid "5da223d5-ac9d-59a3-bcda-b66c2be933a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-66074" :question/problem "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x-{60}^{∘}),$ where $x$ is measured in degrees? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ consists of all values where $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\ne 0,$ and is given by the expression\n\n$$\nx\\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x-{60}^{∘}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(3x-{60}^{∘}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n3x-{60}^{∘}\\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-{60}^{∘} & \\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ 3x & \\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}+{60}^{∘} \\\\ 3x & \\ne {150}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x & \\ne {50}^{∘}+n\\cdot {60}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne {50}^{∘}+n\\cdot {60}^{∘},$ where $n$ is an integer." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-66074/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ec59516-4ba6-5e62-b26a-bad36ea4448f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-66074"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-66087/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "e036d14f-0ff9-5080-a743-fff0bb7fc1e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-66087/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "058fbcdd-dd21-557c-8cfd-bee11b2fda89" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-66087/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "8841341b-0412-5c16-a615-9fd2bb055091" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-66087/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "a1722eae-ef03-506a-b22b-4dcc68c9bd0d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-66087/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer/id #uuid "6d99fcb4-c4c0-5442-8e8b-b03a9bf7fd4a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-66087/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5e90bbce-22bc-5a9c-9520-bfbfb49f5446" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-66087/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer-field/choices ["q-66087/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-66087/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-66087/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" "q-66087/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-66087/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-66087" :question/id #uuid "04a71c1a-0ab2-549e-ba81-a58fecdbea24" :question/math-academy-id "q-66087" :question/problem "What is the value of $f(x)=-2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+4$ at its local minimum points?" :question/worked-solution "On a negative branch of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\le -1.\n$$\n\nThe points where $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-1$ are **local maximums** of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$ For now, we're interested in the local maximums (not local minimums) of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ because we see that $f(x)$ contains a vertical reflection.\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\le -1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-2$ and adding $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\le -1 \\\\  & -2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\ge 2 \\\\  & -2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+4\\ge 2+4 \\\\  & -2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+4\\ge 6 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=6$ at its local minimum points." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-66087/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5085a30f-a347-5ba8-80fd-102e4e0f37ce"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-66087"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
